WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.570
Hello everyone and welcome back to EUpeople

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.800
Today we connect with Roberto Viola

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:09.880
a techie at the head of the digital department of the European Commission

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:13.140
Together we will browse EU digital policies

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:18.300
and see how decisions taken
today will affect your life tomorrow!

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:23.380
We will also navigate his past, pick up some old phones and plug into satellites...

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:28.420
Your Wi-Fi is working? Your phone is fully
charged? Then let's begin!

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:44.500
I am delighted to welcome
Roberto Viola, the digital guru of the European Commission.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.200
Some say he only sleeps four hours and a half a day

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:51.140
Others that he has brain hardwired for technology.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.880
The man who once gave powerful people a headache is full of mysteries...

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.820
But one thing is for sure:

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.440
Roberto's work will shape the digital world for the next 20 years, at least.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:04.160
How, you ask? Let’s find out.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.420
Good morning Roberto! Good morning Romain

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.020
Would you call yourself a digital guru?

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.480
I would say a digital savvy...

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.420
I've seen a picture of you building a computer in the 80s... how come?

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:20.500
It was the time of the first computers...

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:25.020
They were the first personal computers coming on the market

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.360
and it was common for the digital
gurus at that time to build their own computers

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.340
I became rather successful: all
my family and friends were asking for computers

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:40.400
I could have open a
business!

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:47.300
The house was full of pieces of
computers, memories.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.320
I didn't know we could build a computer even!

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.840
You could do this. Still you can do it nowadays. It's not that common.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:00.500
Could you tell us in few simple words: what is the digital single market ?

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:08.060
Oh it's a beautiful thing! In Europe you can move around and you don't feel you're changing country.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:12.560
You don't pay roaming fees. You have the same rules for privacy. You can shop online.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.120
You can watch your favorite movies, your
favorite sports across borders.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.940
How do you do with evolving technologies:

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:28.060
I guess there's a balance to make between regulation and also letting the technology blossom

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.980
So how do you keep this balance?

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:33.600
It's very difficult and you have to keep this balance indeed.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:41.140
You have to allow people to innovate to
really disrupt all the things and create new ones.

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:44.440
At the same time people also need to
be protected.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.560
There should be transparency for the customers.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:52.320
The rights of the people have to be respected: privacy, individuality.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.780
I mean, you always have to keep this fine balance.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:01.580
To make it understandable to
people: could you explain me some key words

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:07.880
in one sentence and what the EU is doing in these fields.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:12.880
So let's start with "Internet of Things"?

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:20.180
Well it's everything connected to the Internet: your fridge that tells you to buy milk when it is finished

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:22.760
Do you have a smart fridge?

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.820
Not yet, not yet...I'll build one!

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:29.760
And what the EU is doing?

00:03:30.360 --> 00:03:34.060
The EU is fostering this technology, its
standardization, its safety

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:38.750
to make sure for instance that your
own camera is not spying you

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:41.280
and at the same time is useful to protect your home.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.360
Artificial intelligence?

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:49.480
Well, it's a computer that can understand things from many different data and things that are

00:03:49.489 --> 00:03:53.930
happening around and gives you a
prediction of what is going to happen.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:57.080
And the EU in this field?

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:02.200
The EU is doing many things. It is basically introducing artificial intelligence everywhere

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:05.659
for the benefit of the citizens

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:09.540
financing research and also working
on ethics.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:11.180
Big data?

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:16.240
It's a big 0 and big 1- I'm joking of course.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.980
Big data is zillions of data that we generate with our smart watch, smartphone,

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:26.320
This sea of data can be searched by artificial intelligent machines

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:33.100
and allow you to understand much better how things work.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.720
Geo blocking?

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:36.420
You felt it I'm sure.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:43.540
Have you tried to shop online and at the
end the website says "sorry I don't ship in your country"

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.240
Like for Christmas...

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:49.860
It's when you tried to rent a car and you were redirected to a website you didn't want to go...

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.560
These are forms of geo blocking.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:54.720
And the EU is trying to prevent it?

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.259
It has abolished it. There is an EU regulation abolishing geoblocking.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:05:03.700
I mean from Christmas onwards, people will be free to shop online wherever they like.

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.830
And what happens... if not?

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:08.540
If you're still geo blocked?

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:11.260
If you're still geoblocked, call me!

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.060
Ok, so we're going to put the phone number of Roberto Viola on screen...

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:17.680
Cybersecurity?

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:25.480
It's protecting online everything which has to do with the movement of data

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:28.580
and making sure that people don't spy on your computer or your smartphone

00:05:28.580 --> 00:05:32.940
don't attack banks or hospitals.
It's a very serious thing.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:35.840
And fake news?

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:38.260
We call it disinformation.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:43.740
It's all the attempts to alter equitable ways of representing information

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:47.240
by people that don't have really good intentions.

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:50.400
Can we make the digital world ethical?

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:55.520
Can we apply online the same rules that we have offline?

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.880
Absolutely! What applies offline you should apply online.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:03.000
So how you behave online should be how you behave offline.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:09.380
People sometimes feel they can
go online and insult others and misbehave

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:10.980
I don't understand why.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:17.740
You should be as ethical when you are offline and when you use your digital self.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.120
Roberto, you remember your first mobile
phone?

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.400
We found it in an old drawer!

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:35.280
You remember how long it took to
type a message and we were limited in characters

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:39.980
So I'd like to start with a
small challenge...

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:45.180
Would you be able to draft the full
regulation on geoblocking on this small phone?

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:51.380
Or alternatively, let's draft "love the digital single market"

00:06:51.380 --> 00:06:52.460
ah okay!

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:54.160
I'm gonna do it on mine...

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:55.340
That's unfair!

00:06:55.520 --> 00:06:57.520
We'll see! Maybe you'll be quicker...

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:02.920
So shall we? Let's try... Are you
ready?

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:04.520
Start!

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:15.060
I have the autocorrect on...

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:16.840
But that's really unfair!

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:19.700
I'm done!

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:20.560
Done!

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:25.700
Two minutes! I can tell: it's correct!

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:28.220
How was it to play with the phone?

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:29.860
Quite a challenge I must say...

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:33.680
More seriously you were at the head of the European telecom regulators

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:38.220
when mobile phone were booming. How was this experience?

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:44.340
You know when I started as a
regulator, there were no smartphones and no tablets.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:49.940
To connect to the internet you had to use what they were calling dial-up modem

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:58.040
you had to dial up a number and they were making strange noises when they were connecting

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:02.360
It was quite a different time!

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:09.320
You couldn't connect to the Internet and at the same time use your phone to call...

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:13.460
But what were you regulating at that
time then?

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:18.840
It was the beginning of the most important thing: the introduction of 3G and then 4G

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:26.360
Regulations to allow cheap prices to connect to the internet thanks to EU rules by the way

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:29.380
I have some questions from the online audience about phones

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:38.500
So we got Garrett who is asking: "what does actually roaming mean?"

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:47.680
It's not a difficult one. Roaming it's using your mobile phone when you are outside your home country coverage.

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:50.740
And the EU did a lot to lower prices?

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:56.940
No zero prices! When you are around in Europe you don't pay roaming fees anymore.

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:02.220
Another question from Yaonnis: Europe developed the GSM standards

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:07.180
and today is lagging behind Asia and the U.S. in developing 5G networks

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:11.440
Is it too bureaucratic to adopt new
technologies?

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:16.600
Look: 5G has not yet started so let's give Europe a chance!

00:09:16.600 --> 00:09:27.960
We are working hard to help researchers and companies deploy 5G very quickly.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:30.960
Okay it's not only about regulation it's also about innovation...

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:33.000
It's a lot about innovation as well!

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:38.300
A question from Gerhardt: "why can't Europe create tech giants?"

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:43.580
It's a question that we hear a lot: tech giants like the US or China?

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:52.880
Europe has many industrial giants. It's fair to say it woke up a bit late to the digital revolution

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:57.630
but now we are very serious
so stay tuned I would say!

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:02.700
Where would you think the tech giant's
would be? In which sectors?

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:09.540
I would say digital finance, in robotics, in
healthcare.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:14.540
I'm sure there will be digital world leaders.

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:21.580
An interesting question: "why are mobile phone bills much higher in the U.S. than in Europe?"

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:27.620
I would say it's a combination of factors.

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:31.380
Mostly in Europe you have more competition.

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:34.040
More competition brings lower
prices?

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:34.940
As always.

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:44.940
Roberto, I'd like to talk about your first
love: space!

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:50.980
You joined the European Space Agency at the age of 25. Did you have the Star Wars syndrome?

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:55.560
Well, probably the Apollo one because
when I saw the moon landing

00:10:55.780 --> 00:11:00.780
and I was 11 at that time I said: that's something.

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:04.840
So you studied in that direction and it was really your focus?

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:08.380
I would say my generation had a special love for space

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:15.600
More seriously, I see you brought us a nice miniature of a satellite and it says it's "Galileo"

00:11:15.600 --> 00:11:17.200
What is Galileo exactly?

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:25.900
In a word, it's the European GPS. It's a constellation of satellites that allows you to know where you are.

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:28.790
And it's used for technologies as well. Is it used for mobile phones?

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:34.520
It's in mobile phones and it
will be a bit everywhere.

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:41.500
It's a new generation of navigation satellites. It will be even more precise.

00:11:41.500 --> 00:11:44.340
Why should Europe have its own
satellites?

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:50.160
First of all Europe needs to have its own satellites because they are used to watch football matches...

00:11:50.660 --> 00:11:55.200
What about this! Is it convincing enough?

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:04.200
We have a very good coverage when it comes to satellite television and telecommunication

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:08.280
Now navigation or satellites monitoring the environment

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:15.740
that's super important to understand what is happening whenever there's a natural disaster

00:12:15.740 --> 00:12:22.160
to have images to understand and to rescue people

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:26.720
So in many different ways satellites are important.

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:29.560
But there's another important reason why space matters

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:33.940
Space technology is a sort of frontrunner technology.

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:39.140
When you move your phone and the
orientation changes: that's called gyroscope

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:43.980
It was invented for space!
or the touchscreen.

00:12:43.990 --> 00:12:50.089
So all the new things you are now using they were already used 20 years ago in space.

00:12:50.089 --> 00:12:54.760
That's why I'm a little bit digital savvy now
because I have seen it all some time ago.

00:12:54.760 --> 00:13:00.680
What is Europe's future in space? What's the next step: do we go to Mars?

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:06.280
We already went I mean but there are
many things to do many things to do...

00:13:06.290 --> 00:13:13.759
The universe is big. Our earth needs
to be understood much better

00:13:13.759 --> 00:13:19.730
and we need to continuously evolve
with new technologies

00:13:19.730 --> 00:13:22.080
Thank you very much Roberto, thank you for connecting with us

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:28.900
I have the impression we had so many topics to cover that it could be a never-ending discussion

00:13:28.910 --> 00:13:33.379
If you want, I have a tent here so we can continue...

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:35.560
Or we could do many episodes if you wish

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:37.000
Thank you everyone for watching

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:40.400
You can comment, share online, you can rewatch the video

00:13:40.460 --> 00:13:44.680
There is a last little game we're gonna play with Roberto about European movies

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:48.560
So I suggest you click on this link: it's a bonus video.

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:49.900
Thank you again Roberto!

00:13:49.900 --> 00:13:51.400
Thank you very much!

